From c6feddb6b64e1ae70e0248b366784543d46935db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kel Modderman <kelvmod@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:43:16 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] FFmpegImage: Convert pictures with an ICC profile to sRGB

Kodi draws pictures as sRGB, so a photo whose ICC profile describes a
wider color space shows duller than it should. In a Display P3 photo, as
a Pixel saves with "Rich colour" on, P3 (200, 100, 100) is sRGB
(215, 93, 97), but Kodi draws it as sRGB (200, 100, 100).

FFmpeg attaches the ICC profile of JPEG, PNG (without a cICP chunk) and
WebP pictures to the decoded frame, and that of HEIF and AVIF ones from
the container. When Kodi is built with lcms2, convert the scaled RGB32
pixels from that profile to sRGB, with relative colorimetric intent.
Profiles that match sRGB and carry no LUT, and gray, CMYK and other
non-RGB ones, are left alone, so sRGB-tagged pictures only cost a
profile parse and untagged ones nothing. Image decoder add-ons are not
covered, nor color given as primaries without a profile (nclx, cICP).

The test pictures are flat 32x32 JPEGs with profiles that lcms2 builds,
written by tools/testdata/mkicc.py.

Assisted-by: Claude
